a for the day and the and a  loop and I should would not be I would  be doing a great disservice if I did not  acknowledge a few people who over the  course of the semester have really tried  to bring all this together so as all of  you know and you probably have the first  person that who I think all of you have  encountered in some form is hanan al  Dudley who's sitting right in the very  front and  [Applause]  I don't I not give making it an  understatement in saying that this would  not have been the way the program is  developed even in its form over the last  several weeks and months would not have  been possible without announced efforts  we really wanted someone who could step  up and help to just convene people and  talk to people from across Gale and anon  has been able to do that we and they  think again we'll see much more but we  need to start with by thanking on for  all of our efforts the others who I need  to acknowledge are not in the room but I  want to at least let you know that the  website and the communications and other  aspects of McMillan's work was put  together by Marilyn Wilks who is the  director for communications as well as  Lisa Brennan who is the webmaster and  web designer for the Macmillan Center  and apart from within Macmillan the  other people I need to mention are  they're there again how we've approached  the program is to draw in people from  across Gale and there are faculty and  centers and units at the law school at  the Jackson Institute at the Medical  School in public health nursing and a  number of other schools and faculties  across Yale that without their support  that's everything that we've done over  the last semester would not have been  possible so we're grateful to all of  them and finally we're grateful to all  of you those of you who are presenting  those of you who are listening and  contributing in your own ways and and  what I would encourage you to do and not  only today but over the coming days and  weeks  it just is continue to give us your  feedback about what more you would like  to see the program do and how you would  like to see it shaped as much as we are  the Macmillan Center is bringing is  serving this convening role we also  regard our work is very organic it is  not we don't have a template and a and a  road map already sketched out rather we  try to be responsive and we want to be  responsive to individual faculty as well  as the interest of others across the  campus so with that let me say thank you  and welcome and if you have any  questions about Macmillan or about the  program do not hesitate to reach out to  her nun or to myself and and we really  look forward to an exciting day of  discussions and presentations so with  that let me turn the the podium over to  Professor Kathryn Patrick a professor  birack is a professor of anthropology as  well as health and global affairs she  holds an appointment in the Jackson  Institute for Global Affairs as well as  the department's of anthropology and the  Macmillan Center and the Faculty of Arts  and Sciences she's a medical  anthropologist  trained both in human biology as well as  in the social sciences she in addition  to the work that she's been doing with  the program she also directs within  Macmillan Center the program on conflict  resilience and health as well as the  anthropologist program on stress and  family resilience the full profile for a  professor Patrick is in your material so  I'm not going to take any more time  other than to welcome her to the podium  to give her remarks thank you good  morning morning so it's my turn to have  the privilege of welcoming you today can  you hear me yes  so today in terms of opening comments to  open up the conversation in room I would  like us to reflect together on one  question that is important to me and  that question is how we can strive to be  all of us better humanitarians each of  us I think has something to offer  perhaps to offer to research or to  practice auto policy I think each of us  has something to offer to have as a  teacher as a student as an advocate have  a fundraiser as an administrator as an  artist even perhaps as a potential donor  by being present in this room each of us  is declared in interest and humanitarian  studies and in humanitarianism and  refugee studies so I think we're here to  reflect on the next generation of  humanitarian  those challenges that we face today and  the challenges that we are likely to  face in the future which echo those we  face in the past we reflect in order to  grasp how we made deepen our  understanding of challenging situations  and how we may seize on opportunities to  develop a more reflective effective and  relevant agenda for making it for doing  things to make a substantial difference  in the world today it's important to  know there's already been much  innovation in the field of refugee  studies moving forward I'd like us to  consider what we can do to be better  humanitarians as researchers  practitioners policymakers or activists  or teachers so I would like us to use  today as an opportunity to make a  difference in our understanding of  humanitarian values and humanitarianism  is encompasses the field of research in  the field of practice and those two  dimensions research and practice are  under twined in a context of exceptional  suffering violence chaos orphan failure  and discrimination humanitarians are  those who save lives and alleviate  suffering and support recovery but I  think that humanitarians are also those  who think deeply about questions of our  issues of sufferings vulnerability and  dignity and surely that includes us in  this room humanitarianism emphasizes I  think a moral dimension of a shared  humanity a practice of compassion that  extends to all human beings and  historically this humanity Essex has  been including for instance in Europe in  18th and 19th century social reforms  that prohibited the use of torture  cruelty to children the abolition of  slavery inhumane treatment of the  mentally ill reforms that fostered  participations justice for women and  other disenfranchised groups so I think  my point here is that this ethic of a  shared humanity has articulated in a  cascade of human rights and social with  reforms have propelled change is driven  change in the field of law health and  politics in the past and I think with  poet poised today to have to witness  more efforts to drive and usher in  another wave of social ethical political  legal health reforms to address a broken  refugee system which is the phrase that  Alex Delaney cough former UN deputy High  Commissioner for Refugees used in his  talk last year a broken refugee system  far forward to international  humanitarian action in 2017 we have had  strong calls made for robust  humanitarian responses to address  comprehensively the needs of refugees  and the concerns of states who yield  power over the lives and the livelihoods  we have had many academic papers that  have been written on the future of  refugee studies but because this is a  very fast changing field we need to take  a moment to reflect carefully on our  agenda so today I think is about our  capacities for critical reflection and  determination being a better  humanitarian means that we work to  define our expectations of what is the  right thing to do in situations of  suffering and injustice you'll see in  today's program session addressing legal  issues the challenges of current  humanitarian systems the rebuilding of  health systems the everyday lives of  refugees and specific examples of  humanitarian actions I hope we're going  to hear today more about research  priorities about partnerships  innovations and accountability because I  personally remember writing in 2015 the  introduction to an edited book or  medical  and sitting in my kitchen and writing  that a plea to inspire innovation  accountability and relevance in this  field and I love those words innovation  accountability and relevance because I  love those words I'm very interested in  them such showcase examples of  innovative humanitarian practice effort  to better communicate the lived  experience of refugees and address  issues of suffering and dignities ways  to forge new approaches to partnerships  between research institutions and  humanitarian organizations ways to share  relevant information and practices so  today I would think is about building a  foundation for grounded mindful and  constructive dialogue between science  research science social science and  humanitarian practice for me being a  humanitarians is to focus on the dignity  of the lack of lives and the robustness  of legal health and political systems  being a humanitarian to hold an ethic of  compassion and care it's also to insist  on accountability in what we do and how  we implement that duty of care as  humanitarian researchers teachers and  activists we need to hold ourselves  accountable to producing or for  producing truthful representations of  people's lives and for creating better  ways of serving them so I think we're  here today to learn from each other to  innovate to build better evidence I hope  we put in place the next generation of  data gathering work partnership building  forward-thinking policy and teachable  insights for humanitarian practice and I  thank the Macmillan Center for being a  catalyst in getting us together  for this symposium today thank you  [Applause]  [Music]
 

